WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.350
Maryland adopted the NAIC long-term care model regulation in COMAR Title 31, Subtitle 14.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:09.936
Insurers must establish marketing procedures that prevent twisting, high-pressure tactics and cold

00:00:09.936 --> 00:00:14.140
lead advertising, and must assure that comparisons are fair and accurate.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:19.108
At or before application Theo must give Gloria the long-term care consumer guide,

00:00:19.108 --> 00:00:24.075
the shopper’s guide, and an outline of coverage that describes benefits, exclusions, renewability

00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:29.043
and premium increase history in a standard format, along with the disclosure titled

00:00:29.043 --> 00:00:32.865
Things You Should Know Before You Buy Long-Term Care Insurance.

00:00:32.865 --> 00:00:35.540
Advertising must be filed with the Commissioner.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.483
Long-term care insurance is expensive and may be held for decades,

00:00:39.483 --> 00:00:43.427
so Maryland requires each insurer to adopt suitability standards and to

00:00:43.427 --> 00:00:46.653
use them in deciding whether to issue a policy.

00:00:46.653 --> 00:00:49.521
Producers must apply the same standards in marketing.

00:00:49.521 --> 00:00:53.106
Before or at application the applicant completes the long-term care

00:00:53.106 --> 00:00:56.691
insurance personal worksheet, covering premium as a share of income,

00:00:56.691 --> 00:00:59.917
assets and the applicant’s understanding that premiums may rise.

00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:04.936
The insurer reviews the worksheet; if the purchase appears unsuitable, it may decline or

00:01:04.936 --> 00:01:09.597
ask the applicant to confirm in writing that she wants the policy anyway.

00:01:09.597 --> 00:01:13.540
The insurer must also ask about existing coverage and Medicaid eligibility.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:19.665
Gloria has 30 days after delivery to return a long-term care policy for

00:01:19.665 --> 00:01:25.791
a full refund, and the policy must say so on its first page.

00:01:25.791 --> 00:01:32.387
If the policy replaces existing coverage, the producer gives a replacement notice, the insurer

00:01:32.387 --> 00:01:38.984
credits time served and waives pre-existing condition periods for benefits the old policy covered,

00:01:38.984 --> 00:01:44.638
and the producer is paid only a renewal commission on the replacement.

00:01:44.638 --> 00:01:50.292
Every individual long-term care policy must be guaranteed renewable or noncancelable; a

00:01:50.292 --> 00:01:55.475
conditionally renewable or optionally renewable long-term care policy is not permitted.

00:01:55.475 --> 00:02:01.600
Group policies must offer continuation or conversion when a member leaves the group.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.628
Insurers must disclose that premiums may increase and must report their rate increase history.

00:02:07.628 --> 00:02:12.364
Incontestability is graduated: within six months the insurer may rescind for

00:02:12.364 --> 00:02:17.100
a material misrepresentation; between six months and two years only if

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.837
the misrepresentation relates to the condition claimed; after two years only

00:02:21.837 --> 00:02:26.142
if the applicant knowingly and intentionally misrepresented relevant health facts.

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:28.726
Benefits already paid are not recovered.

00:02:28.726 --> 00:02:33.462
To prevent unintentional lapse, the applicant may name a third party

00:02:33.462 --> 00:02:38.198
to receive lapse notices, the insurer must give 30 days’ notice

00:02:38.198 --> 00:02:42.934
before terminating for nonpayment, and a policy that lapsed because of

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:47.240
cognitive or functional impairment may be reinstated within five months.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:52.812
Every insurer must offer inflation protection of at least 5 percent compounded annually and

00:02:52.812 --> 00:02:58.384
may issue a policy without it only when the applicant rejects it in writing.

00:02:58.384 --> 00:03:02.762
A pre-existing condition may be defined no more strictly than one

00:03:02.762 --> 00:03:07.140
treated in the six months before coverage, and may be excluded

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.120
for no more than six months after the effective date.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.294
Insurers must offer a nonforfeiture benefit, usually a shortened benefit period, and must

00:03:16.294 --> 00:03:21.070
provide a contingent nonforfeiture benefit when a substantial premium increase causes lapse.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:25.846
Benefit triggers may be no more restrictive than two of six activities

00:03:25.846 --> 00:03:30.622
of daily living or cognitive impairment, and no policy may require a

00:03:30.622 --> 00:03:35.000
prior hospital stay or condition home care on prior facility care.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.602
Maryland rewards buying long-term care coverage with an income tax credit.

00:03:39.602 --> 00:03:45.041
Under Tax-General Section 10-718, an individual who pays premiums for a qualified long-term

00:03:45.041 --> 00:03:50.479
care policy covering herself, her spouse, a parent or stepparent, or a child

00:03:50.479 --> 00:03:55.918
or stepchild who is a Maryland resident may claim 100 percent of the

00:03:55.918 --> 00:04:00.520
eligible premiums paid that year, up to 500 dollars per insured.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:05.959
The credit is annual, may be claimed by only one taxpayer per insured,

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:11.398
cannot exceed the state tax after other credits and cannot be carried forward.

00:04:11.398 --> 00:04:16.418
It is unavailable if the insured held long-term care insurance before July

00:04:16.418 --> 00:04:21.020
1, 2000 or the credit was claimed for that insured before.
